 "article": {
  "title": "Time Banking: Paying with Hours, Not Money",
  "topic": "社会（社区互助·时间银行）",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 236,
  "paras": [
   "In many cities, a new kind of exchange is quietly growing. Instead of paying for help with money, neighbours pay with time. This idea is called “time banking”, and it is built on a simple rule: one hour of your time is worth one hour of anyone else's.",
   "The system works like this. When you spend an hour helping someone—walking a dog, repairing a bike, or teaching a language—you earn one “time credit”. Later, you can spend that credit to receive an hour of help from someone else. A retired teacher may give maths lessons, and in return get her garden tidied by a young student. Because everyone's hour counts equally, the banker and the cleaner are treated the same.",
   "Supporters say time banks do more than save money. They bring together people who would otherwise never meet, and they give value to skills that the market often ignores, such as listening, cooking or simply being patient. For older people, especially, the system offers both practical help and a reason to stay active.",
   "Of course, time banking is not perfect. It depends on trust, and a small community may not have enough members to match every need. Some people worry that credits will be lost if the group closes. Yet for those who join, the reward is often more than practical: it is the feeling of belonging to a place where help flows in both directions."
  ],
  "zh": "在很多城市里，一种新的交换方式正在悄然兴起：邻居之间不用钱、而是用时间来支付帮助——这就是“时间银行”，它建立在一条简单规则上：你的一个小时，等于任何人的一个小时。它的运作是这样：当你花一小时帮别人（遛狗、修自行车、教一门语言），就获得一个“时间币”；日后你可以用它换取别人一小时的帮助。退休教师可以教数学，作为回报由年轻学生帮她整理花园。因为每个人的一小时都被视为等价，银行家和清洁工受到同样的对待。支持者说，时间银行不只是省钱：它把原本永不相识的人联结起来，也让市场常常忽视的技能（倾听、做饭，或者仅仅是耐心）有了价值；对老年人来说，它尤其既提供实际帮助，也给了他们保持活跃的理由。当然，时间银行并不完美：它依赖信任，小社区可能人手不够、难以满足所有需求，也有人担心一旦团体关闭、时间币就会作废。但对加入的人来说，回报往往超出实用层面——那是一种归属于某个“互相帮忙”的地方的感觉。",
